{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe MCP endpoint became unreachable on 2026-08-28. The container was healthy (local probe:\n`200` in 1.8ms) but external requests timed out, because nginx was buffering to\n`/var/lib/nginx/tmp/` on a root disk at **92% (2.6G free)**. Reclaiming docker garbage took it\nto **57%** and the endpoint recovered immediately.\n\n## Why the automatic reclaim did not self-heal it\n\n`prune_docker_caches()` (`autodeploy.sh:276-284`) runs a capped\n`docker builder prune -f --keep-storage \"$BUILD_CACHE_KEEP\"` plus `docker image prune -f`:\n\n1. `BUILD_CACHE_KEEP` defaults to `5GB` (`:124`) and the cache measured **5.254GB** — the\n   prune retained it BY DESIGN.\n2. `docker image prune -f` without `-a` removes only DANGLING images (`:272` states the\n   intent). Blue-green tags every build with an immutable per-release tag, so the box held\n   **44 images / 11.09GB, 10.08GB reclaimable**. Tagged-image accumulation is the dominant\n   leak and nothing is permitted to remove it.\n\nSo the gate ran on schedule, logged `\"disk pressure reclaim complete\"`, freed ~nothing, then\nthrottled for 600s and repeated that misleading success. `observability.sh:330-335` claims its\nmarker lets an incident sweep *\"distinguish 'the reclaim gate never ran' from 'it ran and\nreclaimed nothing'\"* without host access. It cannot: it counts INVOCATIONS, not bytes freed.\n\n### The actual tag scheme (corrected 2026-08-28)\n\nAn earlier revision called the per-release image `rebar-mcp-<sha>`. Wrong:\n\n* **Image tag** — `\"$MCP_IMAGE:$TARGET\"` = `compose-mcp:<40-char-sha>`, tagged at\n  `autodeploy.sh:763`, consumed by `mcp_run_new` at `:406`. `MCP_IMAGE` defaults to\n  `compose-mcp` (`:146`), so ALL releases share one image NAME and differ only in the TAG.\n* **Container name** — `\"${MCP_CONTAINER_PREFIX}-${TARGET:0:12}-${mcp_newport}\"` (`:783`) =\n  `rebar-mcp-<sha12>-<port>` (`:147`). So `rebar-mcp-<sha>` names a CONTAINER, never an image.\n* **No `compose-mcp:prev` (or explicit `:latest`) is ever created.** `:prev` is a `$BOT_IMAGE`\n  scheme only (`:676`/`:679`/`:689`). The mcp target's rollback is the blue-green mechanism\n  itself: on an unhealthy/storeless new container or a failed flip it `docker rm -f`s the NEW\n  container and leaves the OLD one serving the unchanged upstream (`:801`, `:827`, `:837`).\n\nBecause the container name carries only a TRUNCATED sha plus the port, it must NOT be\nstring-manipulated back into an image tag. The mapping comes from ASKING DOCKER:\n`docker inspect -f '{{.Config.Image}}' <container>` returns the exact `compose-mcp:<sha>`\nreference that container was started from. That is the identifier the retire path removes.\n\nAlerting was not the gap: `rebar-root-disk-pressure` (85%) **did fire** and sat in ALARM ~11\nhours, flapping as the reclaim freed a little and the disk refilled. A threshold alarm cannot\nsay \"reclaim is running and achieving nothing\" — the actionable condition.\n\n## Scope (operator-directed, 2026-08-28)\n\n1. **Retire the per-release `compose-mcp:<sha>` image once its container is fully drained and\n   reaped.** The blue-green retire path (`mcp_retire_sweep`) already reaps exited containers\n   with `docker rm`; removing that container's image belongs there — AFTER the reap succeeds,\n   never while serving. The image reference is read with\n   `docker inspect -f '{{.Config.Image}}' <container>` BEFORE the `docker rm` (it is\n   unreadable afterwards). Two references are retained: the bare `$MCP_IMAGE` build tag (the\n   `docker compose build mcp` output `:763` re-tags each deploy) and the LIVE backend's image.\n   Removal uses `docker image rm` WITHOUT `-f`, so the daemon itself refuses while any\n   container still references the image — an independent second guard.\n2. **Log root-disk free space before and after every reclaim**, plus the delta, so effect is\n   observable rather than inferred.\n3. **Emit a loud, distinct marker when pressure persists across 3 or more reclaim cycles** —\n   the \"reclaim is ineffective\" signal that no existing alarm expresses.\n\n### The streak state machine — one rule per tick outcome\n\n`reclaim_under_pressure` has THREE outcomes, and the counter is defined on each:\n\n1. **Below threshold** — the early return at `:302`. The RESET lives HERE, *before* that\n   return, not next to the increment: a recovered tick never reaches the post-reclaim code, so\n   a reset placed there would be unreachable and the counter would never clear.\n2. **Pressured but THROTTLED** (`:307-310`, within `PRESSURE_PRUNE_MIN_INTERVAL`) — UNCHANGED.\n   It counts reclaim CYCLES, not ticks: a throttled tick ran no reclaim, so it is evidence\n   neither for nor against effectiveness. This keeps the marker honest — at a ~2-min tick and\n   a 600s throttle, counting ticks would reach 3 inside ONE throttle window, firing while only\n   a single reclaim had run.\n3. **Reclaim RAN** — re-read `root_disk_pct()` AFTER the prune. Still `>= DISK_PRESSURE_PCT`:\n   increment. Below it: reset to `0` (the reclaim worked).\n\n### Persistence and configurability\n\nThe gate runs as a SEPARATE process per ~2-min tick, so the counter is persisted on disk,\nmirroring `PRESSURE_PRUNE_TS_FILE` (`autodeploy.sh:133`): a file under `$STATE_DIR` named by an\noverridable `PRESSURE_STREAK_FILE`, written write-tmp-then-`mv` so a torn write is never\nobserved. It **fails safe toward silence** — absent, unreadable, or non-numeric reads as `0`,\nso a lost counter delays the marker rather than emitting a false one. The threshold is a named\noverridable constant `PRESSURE_STREAK_ALARM` (default `3`), consistent with the existing\n`DISK_PRESSURE_PCT` / `PRESSURE_PRUNE_MIN_INTERVAL` tunables — not a hardcoded literal.\n\n### Out of scope: the pre-existing tag backlog\n\nThe reclaim is retire-path-only. Tags orphaned earlier — including any reaped in the crash\nwindow between `docker rm` and the image removal — have no future reap event and are ACCEPTED\nas residue: the backlog was already cleared manually (44 images -> 4), so steady state is\nbounded. An unconditional `docker image prune -a` is NOT adopted — it cannot distinguish the\nlive release from garbage. Draining residue stays a manual operator action.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] After a blue-green container is reaped, the `compose-mcp:<sha>` image it was running is\n      removed, while the live backend's image and the bare `$MCP_IMAGE` build tag are retained\n      (checked by a test that reaps a fake container and asserts exactly which image references\n      survive).\n- [ ] A serving container's image is NEVER removed — the retire path removes an image only\n      after the `docker rm` for that container has succeeded (checked by a test that seeds a\n      RUNNING container and asserts no image removal is issued for it).\n- [ ] `prune_docker_caches` logs root-disk free space BEFORE and AFTER, and the delta, on every\n      invocation (checked by asserting both values and the delta appear in the emitted log line).\n- [ ] When a reclaim completes and the disk is STILL at or above `DISK_PRESSURE_PCT`, the\n      persisted streak counter increments; a below-threshold tick resets it to zero even though\n      it takes the early return; a throttled pressured tick leaves it unchanged (checked by\n      driving the counter across a pressure / throttle / relief sequence).\n- [ ] At `PRESSURE_STREAK_ALARM` (default 3) or more consecutive ineffective cycles, a distinct\n      marker is emitted (not the existing `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE`, which counts invocations)\n      and `observability.sh` publishes it as its own delta metric, so it is alarmable without\n      host access.\n- [ ] The new marker fires only on PERSISTENCE: a single pressured cycle that then recovers does\n      NOT emit it (checked by a negative-control test — this is the discriminator, since an\n      alarm that fires on any pressure is the flapping alarm we already have).\n\n## Evidence\n\nDisk 92%->57%; images 44->4 (11.09GB->2.779GB); build cache 5.254GB->0; endpoint went from four\n12s timeouts to six 401s at ~250ms."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787944212254385001,"uuid":"675057be-1fc0-422c-a5eb-aaff8a9c8653"}